==English==

===Etymology===
From the French Imperial forename ''[[Napoleon]]'', usually with reference to [[w:Napoleon Bonaparte|Napoleon Bonaparte]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/nəˈpəʊlɪən/|lang=en}}
*: {{rhymes|əʊliən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|historical|_|currency|slang|lang=en}} The [[franc germinal]]: a 20-[[franc]] [[gold coin]] [[issue]]d under [[w:Napoleon I of France|Napoleon]].
# {{context|historical|_|currency|slang|lang=en}} [[other|Other]] [[subsequent]] 20-[[franc]] [[coin]]s, [[note]]s, or [[value]]s.
#* '''1891''', {{w|Arthur Conan Doyle}}, ''{{w|The Adventure of the Red-Headed League}}'' (Norton 2005 p.67)
#*: We had occasion some months ago to strengthen our resources, and borrowed, for that purpose, thirty thousand '''napoleons''' from the Bank of France.
# A kind of [[pastry]] of French origin.
#* {{RQ:Vance Nobody|I}}
#*: Little disappointed, then, she turned attention to "Chat of the Social World," gossip which exercised potent fascination upon the girl's intelligence. She devoured with more avidity than she had her food those pretentiously phrased chronicles of the snobocracy […] distilling therefrom an acid envy that robbed her '''napoleon''' of all its savour.

